<title>UK: UK HGCA survey shows cereal, rapeseed plantings up.</title>
<headline>UK HGCA survey shows cereal, rapeseed plantings up.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-05-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Total UK plantings of wheat, barley and oats for the 1997 crop were up by 3.8 per cent to 3.465 million hectares, according to the Home Grown Cereals Authority.</p>
<p>Oilseed rape plantings were up by 6.0 per cent to 378,000 hectares, the HGCA's 1997 plantings survey shows.</p>
<p>The survey shows wheat plantings up by 2.5 per cent at 2.026 million hectares, barley plantings up by 5.7 per cent at 1.338 million hectares and oats plantings up by 4.3 per cent at 100,000 hectares.</p>
<p>According to the HGCA, these figures show that some 150,000 hectares have come back into production following the cut in the set-aside requirement from 10 to 5 per cent.</p>
<p>It also indicate a rise in voluntary set-aside, that is land set-aside above the minimum required, the HGCA said.</p>
<p>--Christopher Lyddon, London newsroom +44 171 542 7928</p>
